To create a high-quality test framework in Cypress that utilizes Docker and the screenshot capabilities of Cypress, follow these steps:

1. Install Cypress and Docker on your development machine. You can follow the official Cypress and Docker installation guides for your operating system.

2. Create a new Cypress project using the Cypress CLI by running the following command:

   ```
   npx cypress create cypress-project
   ```

3. Navigate to the newly created Cypress project directory and open the `cypress.json` file. Add the following configuration to enable Docker support:

   ```json
   {
     "docker": {
       "image": "node:14.17.0-alpine"
     },
     "video": false,
     "experimentalInteractiveTesting": false,
     "screenshotsFolder": "cypress/screenshots",
     "videosFolder": "cypress/videos",
     "chromeWebSecurity": false,
     "pageLoadTimeout": 60000,
     "defaultCommandTimeout": 5000
   }
   ```

   - The `docker` configuration specifies the Docker image to use for running Cypress tests. You can replace `node:14.17.0-alpine` with a different image based on your requirements.
   - The `screenshotsFolder` and `videosFolder` options specify the local directory paths where Cypress will save screenshots and videos respectively.
   - The `chromeWebSecurity` option disables Chrome's same-origin policy, which can be helpful in some scenarios.
   - The `pageLoadTimeout` and `defaultCommandTimeout` options specify the maximum time Cypress will wait for page loads and commands to complete, respectively.

4. Create a new Cypress test file, e.g. `cypress/integration/example.spec.js`, and add the following code to capture screenshots on failure:

   ```javascript
   import { defineConfig } from 'cypress'

   const screenshotOnFail = (on, config) => {
     config.video = false
     config.pageLoadTimeout = 60000
     config.defaultCommandTimeout = 5000
     config.failOnStatusCode: 0
     config.retries = { openMode: 2, runMode: 2 }
     config.reporter = 'mocha-junit-reporter'
     config.reporterOptions = {
       mochaFile: 'results.xml',
       junit: {
         outputPath: 'junit',
         suite: Cypress.config('baseUrl').replace(/^(https?|ftp):\/\/?|[^a-zA-Z0-9_\-\.]/g, '').replace(/\/+/g, '/').replace(/%2F/g, '/').replace(/\/$/g, '')
       }
     }
     config.screenshots = {
       capture: 'onFailure',
       overwrite: true,
       path: 'cypress/screenshots',
     }
     config.metadata = {
       'jobName': Cypress.config('baseUrl').replace(/^(https?|ftp):\/\/?|[^a-zA-Z0-9_\-\.]/g, '').replace(/\/+/g, '/').replace(/%2F/g, '/').replace(/\/$/g, ''),
       'testRunner': 'Cypress',
     }

     on('task', {
       takeScreenshot: (title, width, height) => {
         return Cypress.runner().takeScreenshot(title, { width, height })
       }
     })

     return config
   }

   export default defineConfig({
     e2e: {
       setupNodeEvents(on, config) {
         return screenshotOnFail(on, config)
       },
     },
   })
   ```

   - The `screenshotOnFail` function configures Cypress to capture screenshots on test failures and sets other options like video recording, page load and command timeouts, retries, and reporting options. The `reporterOptions` option specifies the location and naming convention for the JUnit XML test report file. The `metadata` option allows you to add custom metadata to your tests.
   - The `defineConfig` function exports a configuration object that includes the `e2e` block, which specifies the configuration for end-to-end (E2E) tests. The `setupNodeEvents` function is called during Cypress setup and returns the `screenshotOnFail` function with the necessary configuration options.
   - The `takeScreenshot` task allows you to take screenshots programmatically within your Cypress tests.

5. Run Cypress tests using Docker by navigating to your Cypress project directory and running the following command:

   ```
   npx cypress run --spec cypress/integration/example.spec.js --headed --browser chrome
   ```

   - The `--spec` option specifies the path to your Cypress test file.
   - The `--headed` option launches the Cypress GUI for interactive testing.
   - The `--browser chrome` option specifies the browser to use.

6. To capture screenshots programmatically, you can use the `takeScreenshot` task within your Cypress test:

   ```javascript
   it('should take a screenshot', () => {
     cy.visit('https://example.com')
     cy.task('takeScreenshot', { title: 'Example Screenshot', width: 800, height: 600 }).then((image) => {
       // Do something with the captured screenshot
     })
   })
   ```

   This code will capture a screenshot with the title "Example Screenshot" and specified dimensions.

With these steps, you now have a high-quality Cypress test framework that utilizes Docker and the screenshot capabilities of Cypress. You can customize this framework to fit your specific testing needs.

Hier ist eine C++-Funktion, die auf einen `std::string` mit Markdown-formatiertem Text verweist und einen `std::string` mit HTML-formatiertem Text zurückgibt, basierend auf dem [Pandoc-Converter](https://pandoc.org/):

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <pandoc/include/pandoc/include.h>

using namespace std;

string convertMarkdownToHtml(const string& markdown) {
    // Create Pandoc options and input/output files
    PandocOptions options;
    options.input_format = "markdown";
    options.output_format = "html";
    options.output_file = "";
    stringstream ss;
    ss << "md_input" << endl;
    options.input_file = ss.str().c_str();

    // Convert Markdown to HTML
    int ret = pandoc_convert(&options, NULL);

    // Check for errors
    if (ret != 0) {
        throw runtime_error("Pandoc conversion failed");
    }

    // Read HTML output from temporary file
    string html_output;
    ifstream input(options.output_file);
    if (input.is_open()) {
        string line;
        while (getline(input, line)) {
            html_output += line;
        }
        input.close();
    } else {
        throw runtime_error("Failed to open Pandoc output file");
    }

    // Clean up temporary files
    pandoc_cleanup();

    return html_output;
}

int main() {
    string markdown = "# Hello, World! \nThis is a **markdown** string. ";
    string html = convertMarkdownToHtml(markdown);
    cout << html << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

